course: dictionary
lesson: 8076
title: Religion: beliefs and respect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A religion is a set of beliefs about God or gods, shared by a group of people. It often has prayers, festivals and rules for living. Religion can also mean the belief in and worship of God or gods in general.
explain: Read the words around the word for clues. «Christians believe in God, yet freedom of religion means everyone may believe, practise, change or have no religion.» Here religion means a person's right to choose what they believe, and it does not have to be the same as yours.
explain: «Mocking someone's religion or clothes, like a headscarf, is bullying.» The sentence tells you that mocking is bullying, so people's religion should be treated with respect. Respect does not mean agreeing with someone. It means treating them fairly.
fact: Religion is a noun. You can say a religion or religions, but religion on its own is often used without a, as in «freedom of religion».
fact: World religions include Christianity, Islam, Judaism, Hinduism, Buddhism and Sikhism.
fact: A «religious festival» is a festival linked to a religion.
fact: Freedom of religion means everyone may believe, practise, change or have no religion, without being forced or mocked.
fact: Respect does not mean agreeing with someone; it means treating people fairly.
exercise: «Christians believe in God, yet freedom of religion means everyone may believe, practise, change or have no religion.» What does freedom of religion mean? | Religions are banned | *Everyone may believe, practise, change or have no religion without being forced | Only Christians are allowed to have beliefs | Everyone must go to the same place of worship
exercise: «Mocking someone's religion or clothes, like a headscarf, is bullying.» According to this sentence, what is mocking someone's religion? | *Bullying | A religious rule | A place of worship | A religious festival
exercise: Which of these is a world religion? | A headscarf | A festival | *Buddhism | A holiday
test: «Some people eat meat; vegetarians and vegans do not, for reasons of animal welfare, health, the environment or religion.» Why might someone not eat meat? | Because they are sleeping | *For reasons of religion, health, animal welfare or the environment | Because they dislike all food | Because it is against the law
test: «Some words are chosen to include everyone, like "happy holidays" instead of "Merry Christmas".» Why might someone say "happy holidays"? | To make a joke about festivals | Because it is shorter to say | To name one religion only | *To include everyone and avoid naming one religion
exam: If others mock someone's religion, festival or clothes, what should you do? | Ignore it and say nothing ever | *Tell them to stop and tell a teacher | Laugh along so you fit in | Join in the mocking
